#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069973 :tutorial/content "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} & = \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{xy}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!" :tutorial/id #uuid "67b283bf-2859-595a-bacb-ebf32b5f7275" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3379 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
